use std
into topo.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with tp_ is exported). It runs density-based topology optimization (SIMP with a density filter and the optimality criteria update) on the Amni-CAD voxel grid: each iteration JavaScript solves the stiffness problem with its multigrid solver, using the element stiffness scales this module writes, and hands back the node displacements; this module turns them into sensitivities, filters them, updates the densities and finally meshes the optimized shape with surface nets. Every length is in millimeters.

```amni
extern fn tp_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "tp_view_f64";
extern fn tp_view_f32(id i32, xs f32[:]) = "tp_view_f32";
extern fn tp_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "tp_view_i32";
```

Grid layout. The grid has `EX`, `EY`, `EZ` elements along x, y and z; element e is (k times `EY` plus j) times `EX` plus i. Nodes form a grid of `NX` equal to `EX` plus 1, `NY` equal to `EY` plus 1 and `NZ` equal to `EZ` plus 1, with node (i, j, k) numbered (k times `NY` plus j) times `NX` plus i. The 8 nodes of element (i, j, k), with n0 its node (i, j, k), are in this order: n0, n0 plus 1, n0 plus `NX` plus 1, n0 plus `NX`, then the same four plus `NX` times `NY`. A node n has 3 displacement unknowns 3n, 3n plus 1 and 3n plus 2.

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `EX`, `EY`, `EZ`, `NX`, `NY`, `NZ`, `NE` (element count), `NN` (node count), `NO` (filter offsets), `NV` (mesh vertices), `NT` (mesh triangles). Growable f64 arrays: `X` (design density per element), `XP` (physical density per element), `XT` (filtered density per element, before projection), `SP` (projection scratch per element), `XN` (candidate density per element), `DC` (sensitivity per element), `DV` (volume sensitivity per element), `HS` (filter weight sum per element), `CE` (element energy per element), `U` (3 per node), `KE` (576, the 24 by 24 element stiffness, row r and column c at 24r plus c), `PR` (16 parameters), `ST` (12 statistics), `W` (filter weight per offset), `ND` (nodal density per node), `VP` (3 per mesh vertex), `SM` (3 per mesh vertex, smoothing scratch), `VV` (1 per mesh vertex, how much load its cell carries). Growable i32 arrays: `MK` (per element: 0 outside the part, 1 design, 2 kept solid), `NK` (per node: 1 when a support or load touches it), `OF` (3 per filter offset: di, dj, dk), `CV` (per element cell: mesh vertex index or minus 1), `TI` (3 per mesh triangle), `CN` (per mesh vertex, smoothing neighbor count). Growable f32 array: `DN` (per element, the stiffness scale handed to the solver).

Parameters in `PR`: 0 target volume fraction vf, 1 penalty p, 2 filter radius rmin in elements, 3 move limit, 4 minimum stiffness ratio emin, 5 element stiffness factor (Young's modulus times element size, so compliance comes out in newton millimeters), 6 extrusion axis (0 x, 1 y, 2 z, minus 1 none), 7 symmetry axis (0, 1, 2, minus 1 none), 8 grid origin x, 9 grid origin y, 10 grid origin z, 11 element size h, 12 largest projection sharpness beta (0.0 turns projection off), 13 projection threshold eta, 14 iterations between beta doublings. Statistics in `ST`: 0 compliance, 1 volume fraction of the part kept, 2 largest density change, 3 grayness (4 times the mean of xp times 1 minus xp over design elements), 4 design element count, 5 kept element count, 6 lagrange multiplier, 7 iteration count, 8 current beta (0.0 when projection is off).

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` does the same for an i32 growable array, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. `z32(xs, n, x)` does the same for an f32 growable array with x as f32. Every array in this file is sized only through these three helpers or by push; no block defines other sizing helpers.

The exported function `tp_views()`, declared with the export keyword, registers every array JavaScript reads or writes: f64 views 1 `X`, 2 `XP`, 3 `U`, 4 `KE`, 5 `PR`, 6 `ST`, 7 `VP`, 8 `CE`, 13 `VV`; i32 views 9 `MK`, 10 `NK`, 11 `TI`; f32 view 12 `DN`; through tp_view_f64, tp_view_i32 and tp_view_f32.

The exported function `tp_grid(ex, ey, ez)`, declared with the export keyword, takes three i32 element counts, sets `EX`, `EY`, `EZ`, `NX`, `NY`, `NZ`, `NE` (ex times ey times ez) and `NN` (`NX` times `NY` times `NZ`), sizes with the helpers `X`, `XP`, `XT`, `SP`, `XN`, `DC`, `DV`, `HS` and `CE` to `NE` zeros, `MK` and `CV` to `NE` zeros, `DN` to `NE` zeros, `U` to 3 times `NN` zeros, `NK` to `NN` zeros, `ND` to `NN` zeros, `KE` to 576 zeros, `PR` to 16 zeros, `ST` to 12 zeros, empties `VP`, `SM`, `VV`, `TI` and `CN`, sets `NV` and `NT` to 0, then calls `tp_views` and returns `NE`.

`node0(e)` is the node (i, j, k) of element e with i equal to e mod `EX`, j equal to (e div `EX`) mod `EY` and k equal to e div (`EX` times `EY`), numbered as above. `enode(e, a)` is node a (0 to 7) of element e in the order given above.

The exported function `tp_keep(layers)`, declared with the export keyword, takes an i32 layer count. Every element with `MK` equal to 1 that has at least one of its 8 nodes with `NK` equal to 1 gets `MK` equal to 2. Then, layers minus 1 more times, every element with `MK` equal to 1 that shares a face with an element marked 2 in the previous pass becomes 2 (the six face neighbors at plus or minus 1 in i, j or k inside the grid; collect the new ones first, then mark them, so one pass grows one layer). It returns the number of elements with `MK` equal to 2.

The exported function `tp_start()`, declared with the export keyword, prepares the run after JavaScript filled `MK`, `NK`, `PR` and `KE`. It counts design elements (`MK` 1) into nd and kept elements (`MK` 2) into nk and writes them to ST[4] and ST[5]. X[e] is 1.0 for kept elements, 0.0 outside the part, and for design elements the larger of 0.0 and the smaller of 1.0 and (vf times (nd plus nk) minus nk) divided by the larger of 1 and nd. It builds the filter offsets: r is PR[2] (at least 1.0), m is the ceiling of r minus 1; for every di, dj and dk from minus m to m whose distance (square root of the sum of their squares) is below r, it pushes di, dj, dk to `OF` and r minus that distance to `W`; `NO` is the number of offsets. For each element e in the part (`MK` above 0), HS[e] is the sum of the weights of the offsets whose neighbor element (i plus di, j plus dj, k plus dk) is inside the grid and in the part. Then it calls `filt` on `X` into `XT`, sets ST[8] to 1.0 when PR[12] is above 0.0 and to 0.0 otherwise, calls `proj` on `XT` into `XP`, calls `scales`, sets ST[7] to 0 and returns nd.
`proj(src, dst)` takes two f64 growable arrays by reference and applies the Heaviside projection with b equal to ST[8] and t equal to PR[13]: for each element e, when `MK`[e] is 0 dst[e] is 0.0, when it is 2 dst[e] is 1.0, and for design elements dst[e] is src[e] when b is not above 0.0, otherwise (tanh(b times t) plus tanh(b times (src[e] minus t))) divided by (tanh(b times t) plus tanh(b times (1.0 minus t))), using Math.tanh.
`dproj(x)` is the derivative of that projection at a filtered density x: 1.0 when ST[8] is not above 0.0, otherwise b times (1.0 minus the square of tanh(b times (x minus t))) divided by (tanh(b times t) plus tanh(b times (1.0 minus t))).

`filt(src, dst)` takes two f64 growable arrays by reference and applies the density filter: for each element e, when `MK`[e] is 0 dst[e] is 0.0, when it is 2 dst[e] is 1.0, and when it is 1 dst[e] is the sum over the offsets whose neighbor element is inside the grid and in the part of the offset weight times src at that neighbor, divided by HS[e].

`scales()` writes `DN`: for elements outside the part 0.0, otherwise emin plus XP[e] raised to p times (1 minus emin), with emin PR[4] and p PR[1], stored as f32.

The exported function `tp_sens()`, declared with the export keyword, runs after JavaScript put the displacements of the latest solve into `U`. For each element e in the part it gathers ue, a local array of 24 reals, where entry 3a plus c is U at 3 times enode(e, a) plus c, for a from 0 to 7 and c from 0 to 2; ce is the sum over r and c below 24 of ue[r] times KE[24r plus c] times ue[c]; CE[e] is ce times PR[5]. The compliance is the sum over the part of (emin plus XP[e] to the p times (1 minus emin)) times CE[e], stored in ST[0]. `XN` holds the raw sensitivities for this step (`tp_update` overwrites it later): for design elements minus p times (1 minus emin) times XP[e] to the power p minus 1 times CE[e] times dproj(XT[e]), and 0.0 for every other element; `SP` holds the raw volume sensitivities: dproj(XT[e]) for design elements and 0.0 otherwise. Then the chain rule through the filter: DC[e] for a design element e is the sum over its in-grid offsets whose neighbor n is a design element of the offset weight times XN[n] divided by HS[n], and DV[e] is the sum over the same neighbors of the offset weight times SP[n] divided by HS[n]; other elements get 0.0 for both. It returns ST[0].

`constrain(xs)` takes an f64 growable array by reference and enforces the shape rules on design elements. When PR[6] is 0, 1 or 2 (extrusion along that axis), each line of elements along that axis gets, at every design element on the line, the mean of xs over the design elements on that line. Then when PR[7] is 0, 1 or 2 (mirror symmetry across the middle plane of the grid square to that axis), each design element e whose mirror element (index along that axis replaced by the count along that axis minus 1 minus the index) is also a design element gets the mean of xs[e] and xs at the mirror, both set at once.

The exported function `tp_update()`, declared with the export keyword, does one optimality criteria step; every int count used in real math is converted with `as real`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls. m is PR[3], target is vf times (ST[4] plus ST[5]). It bisects the lagrange multiplier with l1 equal to 0.0 and l2 equal to 1e9 while (l2 minus l1) divided by (l1 plus l2 plus 1e-30) is above 1e-4 and fewer than 200 rounds ran: lm is the middle; every design element gets XN[e] equal to X[e] times the square root of the larger of 1e-30 and minus DC[e], divided by the larger of 1e-30 and DV[e] times lm, then clamped to at least the larger of 0.0 and X[e] minus m and at most the smaller of 1.0 and X[e] plus m; non-design elements keep XN[e] equal to X[e]. Then `constrain` on `XN`, `filt` from `XN` into `SM`, `proj` from `SM` into `SP`, and the volume is the sum of `SP` over the part; when it is above target, l1 becomes lm, otherwise l2 becomes lm. Every filtered array is sized with `zf` to `NE` before use. After bisection ST[6] is the final lm, ST[2] is the largest absolute difference between XN and X over design elements, X takes XN, `filt` from `X` into `XT`; when PR[12] is above 0.0, ST[8] is below PR[12] and ST[7] plus 1 is a positive multiple of the larger of 1 and PR[14] (both converted with `as int`), ST[8] becomes the smaller of PR[12] and 2 times ST[8]; then `proj` from `XT` into `XP`, ST[1] is the sum of `XP` over the part divided by (ST[4] plus ST[5]), ST[3] is 4 times the mean over design elements of XP times 1 minus XP (0.0 with no design elements), `scales` runs, ST[7] grows by 1, and it returns ST[2].

The exported function `tp_mesh(iso, passes)`, declared with the export keyword, meshes the optimized shape with surface nets and returns the triangle count; iso is a real and passes an i32 (compare it after converting with `as int`; every int used in real math, such as grid coordinates and hit counts, is converted with `as real`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls). Nodal density: ND[n] is the sum of XP over the up to 8 elements around node n that are inside the grid, divided by 8 (elements outside the grid count as 0.0). Node n is inside when ND[n] is above iso. Mesh cells are the elements: for each element cell c whose 8 corner nodes are not all inside and not all outside, a vertex is added at the mean of the crossing points of its 12 edges whose two end nodes differ (edge from node a to node b: the point at fraction t equal to (iso minus ND[a]) divided by (ND[b] minus ND[a]) from a to b, clamped to 0 to 1), in millimeters: x is PR[8] plus the grid x coordinate times PR[11], and likewise y with PR[9] and z with PR[10]; CV[c] holds its vertex index, minus 1 for cells with no vertex. Before the cells, `VV` is emptied and emax is the largest over the part of XP[c] to the power PR[1] times CE[c]; each added vertex also pushes to `VV` the square root of XP[c] to the power PR[1] times CE[c] divided by emax (0.0 when emax is not above 0.0). Then for each grid edge between node a at (i, j, k) and node b one step along an axis whose inside flags differ, with all four cells around it inside the grid, it adds two triangles over those cells' vertices. For an x edge the four cells, as element (i, j, k) indices, are q0 (i, j minus 1, k minus 1), q1 (i, j, k minus 1), q2 (i, j, k), q3 (i, j minus 1, k); for a y edge q0 (i minus 1, j, k minus 1), q1 (i minus 1, j, k), q2 (i, j, k), q3 (i, j, k minus 1); for a z edge q0 (i minus 1, j minus 1, k), q1 (i, j minus 1, k), q2 (i, j, k), q3 (i minus 1, j, k). When node a is inside the triangles are (q0, q1, q2) and (q0, q2, q3), otherwise (q0, q2, q1) and (q0, q3, q2), each pushed to `TI` as the three cells' vertex indices. `NV` and `NT` are set, then `smooth` runs passes times, then `tp_views` runs again.

`smooth()` does one Taubin round on the mesh in `VP` and `TI` (i32 entries of `TI` become indices with `as int` and counts become reals with `as real`): two steps with factor 0.5 then minus 0.53. Each step zeroes `SM` (3 per vertex) and `CN` (1 per vertex), then for every triangle adds each of its corners' positions to the other two corners' sums and adds 2 to each corner's count; every vertex with a count above 0 moves by the factor times (its sum divided by its count minus its position).

The exported function `tp_volume()`, declared with the export keyword, returns the enclosed volume of the mesh in cubic millimeters: the sum over triangles (a, b, c) of the dot product of a with the cross product of b and c, divided by 6.0.

`main()` does nothing.
